 "text": "9/29/26, 9:42 AM\nThe expected meter reading at time P is 1,120 kWh.\n\ne\u00b0 Example\n\nExample 3: Forecasts from previous periods and changes to period consumption values in the base period\n\n2                                     10\nReg. Fact [aman n nn nnn a |\n\nMR Result 0      720 = 800       ? kWh\n|---|\n\nMR Reas.    |                    C      Y                        P\n\n08                           10        02                                01\n4000           9000                                               kWh/Year\n\nPer.Cons... |\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014_\u2014 |] \u2014\u2014_cqrcr_\n\nWght. Unit                300              100              600\n\nWght. Por.               30%            10%             60%\n\nOn installation of the device, a period consumption value of 4000 kWh/year is entered for the register. This value is changed to\n5,000 kWh / year for the period 08 to 10.\n\nThe minimum portion of weighting in percent is 30%.\n\nThe sum of the weighting units for one year is 1,000.\n\nWith a weighting portion of 10%, period O2 to 10 is not representative. The system goes back to the next meter reading result\n08. With 40%, the period 02 to 08 is representative, however, the period consumption was changed within this period.\nTherefore, extrapolation is based on the period consumption rather than on the meter reading results:\n\n1. Determination of period consumption per weighting unit by dividing the period consumption by the weight of validity\nperiod: 5,000 kWh: 1,000 = 5 kWh\n\n2. Extrapolation of period consumption for period 02 to O01: 5 kWh600 = 3,000 kWh\n\n3. Determination of the meter reading difference for period O02 to O1 by dividing the consumption value by the register\nfactor: 3,000 kWh: 10 = 300 kWh\n\n4. Determination of the expected meter reading for O1 by adding the meter reading difference for meter reading 02: 800\nkWh + 300 kWh = 1,100 kWh\n\nThe expected meter reading at time P is 1,100 kWh.\n\n(eo)\n\n*\u00b0 Example\n\nExample 4: Forecasts from previous periods and changes to period consumption values not in the base period\n",
